        body: "The hot-water budget starts with the CIP protocol. Each wash cycle for the milk line and equipment has a defined volume, temperature, and contact time, and a large parlor runs those cycles back-to-back at shift changes — the heater and storage must deliver the second cycle at temperature, not just the first. The engineer totals the CIP draws, adds hose-station and sink use, and applies a diversity factor based on the actual milking schedule rather than a generic commercial multiplier. Storage tanks are sized so recovery happens between milkings, and the engineer checks the coldest incoming water temperature, since winter groundwater can be 20 degrees colder than summer.\n\nHeat recovery from milk cooling is the standard pairing. The refrigeration plant rejects heat exactly when the parlor needs hot water soon after, so a desuperheater or dedicated recovery exchanger preheats the cold feed to the water heaters. The engineer sizes the recovery storage against both the available heat and the draw profile — oversized recovery tanks that never get drawn down waste money, undersized ones leave savings on the table. Distribution gets insulated recirculated loops or heat trace on dead legs, because a hose station 200 feet from the heater that delivers lukewarm water gets the crew reaching for the chemical concentration to compensate, which damages equipment.",
