        heading: "Layout, Finishes, and Clean-Zone Separation",
        body: "The milk house earns its finishes. Floors are sealed concrete, epoxy-coated, or quarry tile — smooth, impervious, and sloped to drain — with coved base at the walls so there is no crevice for milk soil. Walls get fiberglass-reinforced panels or sealed, washable block; the ceiling is a washable, moisture-resistant surface, not exposed structure that sheds dust and grows mold. The engineer details every penetration — refrigerant lines, conduit, plumbing — with sealed escutcheons, because an unsealed pipe chase is a pest and moisture path into the clean zone.\n\nSeparation from the barn is both physical and pressure-based. The milk house door from the parlor or exterior is self-closing and sealed; windows are fixed and screened; and the ventilation design holds the room slightly positive to the parlor so air flows out, not in, when the door opens. The engineer keeps the tanker drive-up on a clean concrete apron with its own drainage, so the milk hauler never tracks barn manure to the tank outlet. Equipment layout follows the work: the tank near the tanker connection, the CIP near the milk-line entry, chemicals in a contained, ventilated corner with spill containment — the inspector's walk path should never cross a dirty-to-clean boundary.",
