        heading: "Service Sizing and Rural Utility Coordination",
        body: "The load list is the foundation. The engineer tabulates every motor and heater — vacuum pumps, air compressors, water heaters, refrigeration condensing units, ventilation fans, milk pumps, lighting, and controls — with nameplate amperage and duty, then determines which run together during milking. That coincident peak, plus a growth allowance the owner actually plans for, sets the service size; feeders and panelboards are sized from the same calculation with voltage drop checked at the farthest parlor motor, because a vacuum pump starting 300 feet from the service on an undersized feeder sags the whole parlor.\n\nRural utility coordination starts early. Many dairy sites sit at the end of long single-phase laterals, and the three-phase service a parlor needs may require a line extension, a larger transformer, or a new tap — with lead times measured in months and costs the owner must budget. The engineer meets the utility with the load calculation in hand, confirms the available fault current for the service equipment ratings, and discusses power quality: rural feeders with frequent recloser operations and lightning exposure justify surge protective devices at the service and at sensitive control panels. Standby generation is coordinated here too — the transfer switch, generator sizing, and load-shedding sequence are part of the same one-line.",
