        question: "Do mature dairy cows need a heated barn?",
        answer: "Generally no. A dry, draft-free mature cow handles cold remarkably well — down toward 20°F at rest — because rumen fermentation generates substantial body heat. Heating the whole freestall barn wastes energy and can actually harm air quality by encouraging tighter, damper buildings. The engineer designs the cow barn for draft-free cold housing with proper winter ventilation, and spends the heating budget on calves, maternity, the parlor, and freeze protection.",
