        body: "Supplemental heat is zoned and choosable. Radiant heaters over group pens let calves thermoregulate by moving under or away from the heat, which beats heating the whole air volume on both energy and animal-health grounds. The engineer sizes the radiant output for the coldest design nights, places thermostats at 2 to 3 feet above the bedding, and interlocks the heaters with the ventilation staging so a heater can't run the pen into a warm, wet, still-air condition. In enclosed calf barns the engineer may add background space heat to keep water lines and the milk-mixing room functional.\n\nBedding dryness is a systems outcome. The engineer slopes pen floors — typically to a central or perimeter drain system — so washdown water leaves completely, details the bedding pack with loader access for full cleanout between groups, and sizes the ventilation to carry off the moisture that bedding and calves release. Condensation control matters: an uninsulated steel roof dripping on calves in winter undoes every other investment, so the engineer specifies enough roof and wall insulation to keep interior surfaces above the dew point at winter design conditions, with a vapor retarder in the right place. Waterers are heated or continuous-flow designs that can't freeze, placed where spillage drains away from bedding rather than into it.",
