        body: "The physiology doesn't negotiate. Heat-stressed dry cows produce less milk in the next lactation, deliver smaller calves, and carry weaker immune function into calving — so the engineer designs dry-barn cooling to the same standard as the lactating barn, not as an afterthought. That means the ventilation strategy (tunnel, cross, or natural with assist) is selected for the dry barn's own geometry and orientation, fan capacity delivers real airspeed over the resting and feeding areas, and inlet cooling — evaporative pads in arid climates — is evaluated on the local wet-bulb data.\n\nSoakers and fans at the feed line deserve specific design. Dry cows still eat at the bunk, and the combination of water soakers wetting the cow's back with high-velocity fan air is the most effective heat-abatement tool available. The engineer sizes the soaker water supply and drainage — soaked water has to go somewhere, and it can't pond in the pen — stages the soakers on temperature with the fans, and details the feed-line area for the wet conditions: non-slip surfaces, drainage, and corrosion-resistant fixtures. Controls are zoned to the dry barn's sensors, because tying dry-barn cooling to the lactating barn's thermostat leaves one group wrong all summer.",
