        question: "Should a freestall barn be insulated?",
        answer: "Usually only selectively. Most freestall barns are cold barns by design — the cows don't need a warm building, they need a dry, draft-free one — so the engineer insulates where it prevents problems: roof planes that would drip condensation on cows, freeze-prone water systems, and the transitions to heated spaces. Fully insulating a freestall barn without re-engineering the ventilation typically creates a warm, humid building with worse air quality than the cold barn it replaced.",
