        heading: "Entries, Quarantine, and Disinfection Infrastructure",
        body: "Personnel entries enforce the protocol in hardware. The Danish entry — dirty-side boot removal, swing over the bench, clean-side boots and coveralls — is detailed with the bench height and layout the crew will actually use, boot wash stations with drainage to the dirty side, storage for clean and dirty gear kept physically apart, and finishes that survive daily disinfection. The engineer sizes entries for shift-change flow so the crew doesn't prop the doors open to beat the queue. Shower-in facilities for higher-security areas get the plumbing, ventilation, and privacy detailing of a small locker room, because a shower nobody uses is just an expensive bathroom.\n\nQuarantine and hospital facilities are sited as the downwind, downstream end of the farm. The engineer places new-animal quarantine and the hospital pens away from and downwind of the main herd and the calf areas, with dedicated water, drainage that doesn't flow through healthy pens, and equipment storage so the hospital tools never migrate to the milking string. Ventilation exhausts are oriented away from other barns' intakes. Mortality management — composting bays or the rendering pickup point — is sited on the dirty route with the county-required setbacks, and the engineer designs the composting pad's drainage and cover per the county's method rules. Vehicle disinfection stations, boot washes, and hand-wash sinks are plumbed, drained, and freeze-protected as permanent infrastructure, not afterthoughts.",
